post_install() {
  cat <<'EOF'
==> On Hyprland, sway and other wlroots compositors Beamer needs a udev rule for each thing it
    does. NEITHER IS IN FORCE: each opens something to every program you run, not to Beamer
    alone, so turn on only what this machine needs. GNOME and KDE Plasma need neither.

==> To BE DRIVEN by another machine: /dev/uinput, to make a virtual keyboard and mouse.
    Every program you run can then type and click as you.

      sudo install -m 644 /usr/share/doc/beamer/70-beamer-uinput.rules /etc/udev/rules.d/
      sudo udevadm control --reload
      sudo udevadm trigger --name-match=uinput

    To take it back:

      sudo rm /etc/udev/rules.d/70-beamer-uinput.rules
      sudo udevadm control --reload
      sudo udevadm trigger --name-match=uinput

==> To DRIVE another machine: /dev/input, to read the keyboards and mice. Every program you
    run can then read every key typed on this machine, passwords included, and take the
    keyboard away from the desktop. Only on a machine that drives others.

      sudo install -m 644 /usr/share/doc/beamer/70-beamer-input.rules /etc/udev/rules.d/
      sudo udevadm control --reload
      sudo udevadm trigger --subsystem-match=input --action=change

    To take it back:

      sudo rm /etc/udev/rules.d/70-beamer-input.rules
      sudo udevadm control --reload
      sudo udevadm trigger --subsystem-match=input --action=change

==> A firewall must let in TCP 24820 and UDP 24821, or pairing fails:
      sudo ufw allow 24820/tcp && sudo ufw allow 24821/udp
EOF
}
